From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:25:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 502/502] [DS4.1] No per-allocation KV zeroing: every cache
 uses only written slots

DeepSeek V4.1's reference zeroes each cache once, when it is built, and
never between sequences: its kernels read only slots a sequence wrote (the
indexer up to end_pos // ratio, the window by -1 indices, the compressor
pooling only complete groups). vLLM instead zeroed every newly allocated
block, because the compressors' FP32 rings beside the byte pages make the
cache mixed-precision, and a reused block's stale bytes of another
precision can decode to NaN.

DS4.1's kernels follow the reference: windows start at the request's
replay start (0501 for the drafter), the sparse MLA never loads a masked
entry, the indexer stores no score past the visible positions (its last
tile's stale rows are discarded), the compressor reads only a ring slot
the request wrote. AttentionSpec.uses_only_written_slots states that no
value of an unwritten slot reaches a result. Every DS4.1 cache declares
it, and needs_kv_cache_zeroing skips mixed-precision caches whose layers
all do. The scheduler then records no block ids and the runner builds no
zeroer for DS4.1; the pool itself is still allocated zeroed. Mamba and
undeclared caches keep zeroing, and VLLM_KV_CACHE_POISON still fills.

The rings' blocks come from the shared pool, so excluding FP32 rings from
the precision rule would be wrong for models whose readers do not bound
their reads; the declaration is per cache.

Tests: the rule with and without the declaration, Mamba and the poison
check (CPU); every spec merge keeps it; DS4.1's own window, compressed,
index and ring specs declare it and skip zeroing.
